# R9E — Plugin Tenant / Organisation Governance

**Status:** APPROVED
**Phase:** R9 — Plugin Governance & External Extension Security
**Predecessors:** R9A, R9B, R9C, R9D
**Next Phase:** R9F — Plugin Governance API & Backend Integration

---

## 1. Purpose

R9E defines how plugins are governed within the hospital/organisation tenant boundary.

The objective is to ensure that:

* a hospital controls which external plugins it may use;
* only authorised hospital administrators can register and approve plugins;
* plugins cannot cross hospital/organisation boundaries;
* plugin configuration belongs to the appropriate hospital;
* plugin execution uses the same platform engine as internal plugins;
* tenant governance does not create a separate execution architecture for external plugins;
* clinical and organisational data remain protected by the existing backend authority model.

R9E builds on the approved decisions in R9A–R9D.

---

# 2. Core Tenant Governance Principle

The fundamental rule is:

> **A plugin may only operate within the hospital/organisation scope for which it has been registered and approved.**

The hospital is therefore the governance boundary for externally introduced plugins.

The platform remains responsible for:

* plugin execution;
* security enforcement;
* validation;
* lifecycle management;
* authorization enforcement;
* runtime isolation.

The hospital is responsible for deciding:

* which external plugins it wants;
* who may register them;
* who may approve them;
* whether an approved plugin remains enabled.

---

# 3. Hospital Administrator Authority

Only an authorised **Hospital Administrator** may perform tenant-level plugin governance actions.

This includes:

* registering a plugin;
* submitting a plugin for approval;
* approving a plugin;
* rejecting a plugin;
* enabling a plugin;
* disabling a plugin;
* requesting removal;
* managing tenant-specific plugin configuration where permitted.

Ordinary healthcare professionals must not be able to:

* upload plugins;
* register plugins;
* approve plugins;
* activate plugins;
* modify plugin governance;
* change plugin tenant ownership.

Frontend controls are presentation only.

The backend remains authoritative for these permissions.

---

# 4. Plugin Ownership

Every externally registered plugin must have an explicit organisational owner.

Conceptually:

```text
Plugin
   ↓
Hospital / Organisation
```

A tenant-owned plugin belongs to exactly one hospital/organisation for its registration instance.

The same plugin software may potentially be registered by multiple hospitals, but those registrations are separate governance records.

For example:

```text
Hospital A
    └── Plugin X
        └── Approved

Hospital B
    └── Plugin X
        └── Pending
```

The approval of Plugin X by Hospital A does not automatically approve it for Hospital B.

---

# 5. Global Plugin Identity vs Tenant Registration

R9E distinguishes between:

### Plugin identity

The identity of the plugin software/package.

and:

### Plugin registration

The relationship between that plugin and a particular hospital.

Conceptually:

```text
Plugin Package
      │
      ├── Hospital A Registration
      │       └── Approved
      │
      ├── Hospital B Registration
      │       └── Pending
      │
      └── Hospital C Registration
              └── Disabled
```

This prevents a hospital's approval decision from becoming a platform-wide approval.

---

# 6. Internal vs External Plugins

The platform supports both internal and external plugins.

### Internal plugins

Examples include:

* WHO knowledge plugin;
* platform-owned knowledge plugins;
* internal prediction plugins;
* other platform-controlled extensions.

These are governed by platform-level ownership and deployment controls.

### External plugins

Examples include:

* hospital-uploaded knowledge plugins;
* hospital-uploaded ML prediction plugins;
* externally supplied plugin packages.

These are subject to tenant registration and approval.

The important architectural rule is:

> **Internal and external plugins ultimately use the same plugin engine and execution framework.**

There must not be a separate "external plugin engine."

---

# 7. Knowledge Plugins

A knowledge plugin provides structured clinical/knowledge information to the platform.

External knowledge plugins may be:

* uploaded by a hospital administrator;
* registered through the plugin governance process;
* approved by the hospital administrator;
* activated only after successful validation and approval.

Knowledge plugins may also originate from a database-backed source.

The platform must distinguish between:

```text
Platform-owned knowledge
```

and:

```text
Hospital-owned external knowledge
```

The WHO plugin remains an internal/platform-controlled knowledge source.

Hospital-specific knowledge plugins must not automatically modify or replace platform-owned knowledge.

---

# 8. Prediction Plugins

Prediction plugins represent machine-learning models.

The project decision is:

> **Prediction plugins are always ML models.**

A prediction plugin therefore represents a model-based computational component rather than a generic rules engine.

Examples may include models for:

* antimicrobial susceptibility prediction;
* resistance risk prediction;
* clinical outcome prediction;
* other approved prediction tasks.

The model/package must be registered and approved before becoming available to the hospital's runtime.

---

# 9. Plugin Upload

R9E supports the approved concept that a hospital administrator may introduce an external plugin through a controlled submission process.

Two submission mechanisms are recognised:

### Option A — ZIP upload

The administrator uploads a plugin package as a ZIP file.

The package contains the required plugin artefacts and metadata defined by R9B/R9C/R9D.

### Option B — Form-based registration

The administrator provides plugin information through a registration form and uploads the required plugin artefact/model.

The form may capture information such as:

* plugin name;
* plugin type;
* version;
* description;
* publisher;
* ownership;
* intended purpose;
* capabilities;
* compatibility information;
* model information for prediction plugins;
* supporting documentation.

The exact UI mechanism is an implementation decision for R9F and must not alter the governance rules established here.

---

# 10. Registration Does Not Equal Approval

Uploading a plugin does **not** make it active.

The lifecycle remains:

```text
Uploaded
   ↓
Registered
   ↓
Validated
   ↓
Pending Approval
   ↓
Approved
   ↓
Enabled
   ↓
Available to Runtime
```

A plugin that fails validation must not reach the enabled state.

A plugin that has been registered but not approved must not execute.

---

# 11. Tenant-Scoped Approval

Approval is always tenant-specific for external plugins.

For example:

```text
Hospital A Administrator
        ↓
Approves Plugin X
        ↓
Plugin X available to Hospital A
```

This does **not** imply:

```text
Hospital B → Plugin X → Approved
```

Hospital B must independently register/approve the plugin if it wishes to use it.

---

# 12. Tenant Isolation

A hospital must never be able to use plugin governance mechanisms to access another hospital's plugins or plugin configuration.

Tenant isolation applies to:

* plugin registration records;
* approval state;
* plugin configuration;
* plugin activation state;
* plugin execution context;
* plugin-generated outputs where tenant-owned;
* plugin audit records.

A hospital administrator must only be able to govern plugins belonging to that administrator's active hospital.

---

# 13. No Frontend Tenant Authority

The frontend must not determine plugin tenancy.

The frontend must not be trusted to supply or override:

* `hospital_id`;
* `organisation_id`;
* tenant ownership;
* plugin owner;
* plugin execution tenant.

The backend derives the authoritative tenant context from the authenticated identity and active membership.

Therefore:

```text
Authenticated User
        ↓
Backend Identity
        ↓
Active Hospital Membership
        ↓
Authoritative Tenant Context
        ↓
Plugin Governance
```

A browser-supplied hospital identifier must never override this resolution.

---

# 14. Plugin Execution Tenant Context

When a tenant-owned plugin executes, the platform must execute it within the authorised tenant context.

Conceptually:

```text
Authenticated Professional
          ↓
Backend Authorization
          ↓
Hospital Context
          ↓
Plugin Registration
          ↓
Plugin Permission/Scope
          ↓
Plugin Execution
```

The plugin must not be able to select another hospital as its execution context.

---

# 15. Plugin Configuration

Tenant-specific configuration belongs to the tenant registration.

For example:

```text
Plugin X
   │
   ├── Global plugin identity
   │
   ├── Hospital A configuration
   │
   └── Hospital B configuration
```

Hospital A must not see or modify Hospital B's configuration.

Configuration must also respect the plugin's approved capabilities and scopes.

A hospital administrator cannot use configuration to grant a plugin capabilities that were not approved by the platform governance/security model.

---

# 16. Plugin Credentials

Plugin credentials must remain governed by the platform.

A hospital administrator must not be able to provide unrestricted platform credentials to a plugin.

Plugin credentials, where required, must be:

* explicitly scoped;
* associated with the appropriate plugin registration;
* limited to approved capabilities;
* protected from ordinary users;
* excluded from frontend exposure;
* auditable.

The detailed credential architecture belongs to R9D/R9F implementation and must not be weakened by tenant configuration.

---

# 17. Clinical Data Boundary

A tenant-owned plugin must not automatically gain access to all hospital clinical data.

Access must be determined by:

* plugin type;
* approved capability;
* approved scope;
* backend authorization;
* tenant context;
* platform security policies.

Therefore:

```text
Hospital ownership
        ≠
Unlimited clinical-data access
```

Hospital approval is necessary for tenant adoption, but it does not override platform security controls.

---

# 18. Knowledge Plugin Boundary

A hospital's external knowledge plugin must not silently override platform-owned clinical knowledge.

The platform should maintain a distinction between:

```text
Platform Knowledge
```

and:

```text
Hospital Knowledge
```

Where both are used in a workflow, the system must retain enough metadata to explain:

* which knowledge source was used;
* which plugin supplied it;
* which hospital owns the plugin;
* plugin version;
* plugin status.

This supports the project's explainability and auditability requirements.

---

# 19. Prediction Plugin Boundary

Prediction plugins must operate within their approved model purpose.

A hospital administrator must not be able to register a prediction plugin as one type of model while using it as an unrelated clinical function.

The registration metadata must identify:

* model/plugin identity;
* model version;
* intended prediction task;
* supported inputs;
* expected outputs;
* compatibility;
* approved capabilities.

The platform remains responsible for ensuring that the model is invoked through the common prediction-plugin engine.

---

# 20. Shared Execution Engine

This is an explicit architectural principle.

> **External plugins must use the same plugin engine, contracts, orchestration, validation and runtime mechanisms as internal plugins wherever applicable.**

The architecture must not evolve into:

```text
Internal Plugin Engine
        +
External Plugin Engine
```

Instead:

```text
                 Plugin Contract
                       ↓
              Plugin Governance
                       ↓
                Plugin Manager
                       ↓
              Common Plugin Engine
                 ↙           ↘
        Internal Plugins   External Plugins
```

The distinction is primarily governance/trust/ownership—not a separate execution architecture.

---

# 21. Hospital Cannot Modify Platform Plugins

Hospital administrators cannot:

* modify WHO;
* replace platform-owned plugins;
* disable mandatory platform safety components;
* modify platform-owned clinical rules;
* alter platform plugin source code.

They may only govern plugins that belong to their tenant and for which the platform permits tenant-level governance.

---

# 22. Plugin Activation

A tenant-owned plugin becomes available to that hospital only when:

1. registration exists;
2. validation succeeds;
3. approval exists;
4. security requirements are satisfied;
5. plugin is enabled.

Conceptually:

```text
Registered
    ↓
Validated
    ↓
Approved
    ↓
Security checks
    ↓
Enabled
    ↓
Runtime availability
```

Disabling the plugin must prevent further use while preserving its governance/audit history.

---

# 23. Revocation

Plugin approval is not permanent.

A plugin registration may become:

* disabled;
* revoked;
* quarantined.

Revocation may occur because of:

* security concerns;
* invalid package replacement;
* failed compatibility;
* malicious behaviour;
* model integrity concerns;
* policy violations;
* administrative action;
* platform-level security action.

A hospital administrator may manage tenant-level activation, but platform security authority may override tenant activation where required for safety.

---

# 24. Platform Security Override

Tenant ownership does not supersede platform security.

Therefore:

```text
Hospital Administrator
        ↓
Tenant governance authority
        ↓
Platform security boundary
```

The platform may disable or quarantine a plugin even if the hospital previously approved it.

This is necessary because plugin code executes within the platform environment.

---

# 25. Cross-Tenant Plugin Sharing

External plugin approval is **not automatically shared across tenants**.

A future platform-level marketplace or centrally approved plugin catalogue could support reusable plugins, but that would require a separate architectural decision.

For R9:

> **Tenant registration and approval are independent per hospital.**

---

# 26. Plugin Removal

Removing a plugin from a hospital must not necessarily destroy its historical governance record.

The system should preserve sufficient information to establish:

* who registered it;
* when it was registered;
* who approved it;
* when it was enabled;
* when it was disabled/revoked;
* which version was used.

This is important for clinical auditability.

---

# 27. Auditability

Tenant plugin governance actions must be auditable.

At minimum, governance events should identify:

* plugin;
* hospital;
* action;
* actor;
* timestamp;
* plugin version;
* resulting state.

Examples:

```text
PLUGIN_REGISTERED
PLUGIN_APPROVED
PLUGIN_REJECTED
PLUGIN_ENABLED
PLUGIN_DISABLED
PLUGIN_REVOKED
PLUGIN_QUARANTINED
PLUGIN_UPDATED
```

The exact persistence schema belongs to R9B/R9F implementation.

---

# 28. Authoritative Governance Hierarchy

The plugin tenant model follows:

```text
Platform Security
       ↓
Platform Plugin Governance
       ↓
Hospital / Organisation Governance
       ↓
Plugin Registration
       ↓
Plugin Activation
       ↓
Runtime Execution
```

A lower level cannot override a higher security boundary.

---

# 29. Relationship to RBAC

Plugin governance must use the existing Phase 16C role and permission architecture.

The system must not create a separate authentication or role system for plugins.

Conceptually:

```text
Supabase Auth
      ↓
Professional Identity
      ↓
Hospital Membership
      ↓
Canonical Role
      ↓
Permission
      ↓
Plugin Governance Action
```

Only the appropriate hospital administrator permission should permit tenant plugin governance operations.

---

# 30. Backend Authority

The backend remains authoritative for:

* tenant ownership;
* plugin registration;
* plugin approval;
* plugin activation;
* plugin configuration;
* plugin execution authorization.

Frontend state is informational/presentational.

No frontend implementation may become a source of truth for plugin governance.

---

# 31. Relationship to R9B

R9B defines the data model required to represent:

* plugin identity;
* tenant registration;
* ownership;
* lifecycle state;
* governance records;
* approval information;
* plugin configuration.

R9E defines the tenant rules those records must enforce.

R9B therefore provides the persistence structure, while R9E provides the organisational governance semantics.

---

# 32. Relationship to R9C

R9C defines the registration and approval lifecycle.

R9E adds the tenant boundary:

```text
Hospital Administrator
        ↓
Tenant Registration
        ↓
Tenant Approval
        ↓
Tenant Activation
```

Approval by one hospital must not affect another hospital.

---

# 33. Relationship to R9D

R9D defines:

* trust;
* integrity;
* execution isolation;
* security controls.

R9E defines **where and for whom** a plugin is allowed to operate.

Together:

```text
R9D
Security / Trust / Isolation
          +
R9E
Tenant / Organisation Governance
```

---

# 34. Explicit Non-Goals

R9E does not define:

* plugin UI design;
* detailed database SQL;
* API endpoint implementation;
* plugin engine implementation;
* ML algorithm design;
* knowledge extraction;
* clinical recommendation logic;
* Supabase authentication redesign;
* R8 session lifecycle;
* R7 invitation redesign;
* plugin marketplace architecture.

Those belong to other phases or future decisions.

---

# 35. Architectural Invariants

The following invariants are mandatory:

### Invariant 1

> A plugin registration belongs to one hospital/organisation.

### Invariant 2

> Only an authorised hospital administrator may govern tenant-owned plugins.

### Invariant 3

> Tenant approval is not global platform approval.

### Invariant 4

> Frontend tenant information is never authoritative.

### Invariant 5

> A tenant cannot grant a plugin capabilities beyond platform-approved security boundaries.

### Invariant 6

> External plugins use the same plugin engine as internal plugins.

### Invariant 7

> Hospital ownership does not imply unrestricted clinical-data access.

### Invariant 8

> Platform security may override tenant activation.

### Invariant 9

> Plugin governance actions must be auditable.

### Invariant 10

> Internal platform plugins remain under platform governance.

---

# 36. R9E Decision Summary

| Decision                                         | Status                       |
| ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------- |
| Hospital is the tenant governance boundary       | **CONFIRMED**                |
| Hospital administrator governs tenant plugins    | **CONFIRMED**                |
| One plugin registration belongs to one hospital  | **CONFIRMED**                |
| Tenant approval is independent                   | **CONFIRMED**                |
| External plugins use the common plugin engine    | **CONFIRMED**                |
| Knowledge plugins may be hospital-provided       | **CONFIRMED**                |
| Prediction plugins are ML models                 | **CONFIRMED**                |
| ZIP/form-based submission supported              | **CONFIRMED**                |
| Frontend is not tenant authority                 | **INHERITED**                |
| Backend derives tenant context                   | **INHERITED**                |
| Platform security can override tenant activation | **CONFIRMED**                |
| Cross-tenant approval sharing                    | **NOT PERMITTED BY DEFAULT** |
| Plugin marketplace                               | **OUT OF SCOPE**             |
| Detailed API implementation                      | **R9F**                      |
| Runtime manager integration                      | **R9G**                      |
| Governance/security tests                        | **R9H**                      |

